  "worked_solution": "We wish to compute the probability that a singer is a rock singer *given that* he/she is a pop singer. Therefore, we need to compute $P(R|P).$ \n\nTo compute $P(R|P),$ we need to ask, \"what is the probability of $R$ occurring given that $P$ has occurred?\"\n\nSince we are assuming that $P$ has occurred, we only consider the singers inside $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273146/assets/d4b01d31b2aa39da2878709417fdc4a348f981f1ce4c2bf3faa40ad6505dc037.png)\n\nIn total, the number of singers in the set $P$ is \n\n$$\n12+22=34.\n$$\n\nOf these $34$ singers, $22$ lie inside $R.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273146/assets/e24ab4d3a89d73e3cf41fbe294d744be4ad100e95d18ee93ea7ba8d442ae3f9a.png)\n\nSince each outcome is equally likely, $P(R|P)$ can be interpreted as the proportion of $R$ that lies inside $P,$ relative to the size of $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|P) & =\\frac{22}{34}=\\frac{11}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
